---
name: post-mortem
description: Close an incident by naming the class of mistake and adding a guard. Use after the platform is serving again, never while it is still down.
---

# post-mortem

This runs after the fire is out. While it is still burning, the only legal work
is putting it out.

A fix that stops one instance is not a fix. Ask what let it break, and keep
asking until the answer names a class rather than one bug.

## 1. The timeline, from commands

```bash
fly releases -a <app> | head -10
gh run list -R {{ github.slug }} --limit 10 \
  --json createdAt,conclusion,displayTitle \
  --jq '.[] | "\(.createdAt[0:16]) \(.conclusion) \(.displayTitle)"'
```

First bad signal, first human notice, fix merged, service restored. Four
timestamps, from output, not memory.

## 2. The class

Write one sentence starting "Any change that ...". If you cannot finish that
sentence, you have a bug, not a class, and you keep asking why.

## 3. Name the rung

The lesson ladder is in `RITUALS.md`. Every post-mortem states a rung:

| rung | what it means |
|---|---|
| 0 | nothing written down |
| 1 | a line in MEMORY.md |
| 2 | a change to a skill |
| 3 | a test in `tests/incidents/` |
| 4 | structural - the mistake is impossible |

Climb as far as the incident allows. If you stop below rung 3, say plainly that
the next occurrence will not be caught.

A lesson reaches memory, a skill or a test **only through a PR the founder
taps**. You propose. He promotes. Never edit MEMORY.md directly from a
post-mortem.

## 4. Close it mechanically, in this order

1. Self-healing - can the system correct itself with nobody involved?
2. A guard - can a machine refuse the mistake? A test, a CI job, a required check.
3. A note - only when 1 and 2 are impossible.

A documented trap is not a guarded trap. If you stop at 3, say plainly that the
next occurrence will not be caught.

## 5. Write it into the eval set

The incident becomes a permanent test. Append one line:

```bash
cat >> $HERMES_HOME/estate-evals/incidents.jsonl <<'ROW'
{"id":"<yyyy-mm-dd>-<slug>","app":"<app>","symptom":"<what was seen>","root_cause":"<why>","class":"Any change that ...","guard":"<path to the test or CI job, or null>","issue":<n>}
ROW
```

Then add the failing case to `tests/incidents/` so it runs on every PR.

## 6. Post it

```bash
gh issue comment <n> -R {{ github.slug }} --body-file <(cat <<'BODY'
## Post-mortem

**Timeline** <four timestamps>
**Root cause** <one sentence>
**Class** Any change that ...
**Guard** <what now refuses it, with a path>
**Not guarded** <what will still get through>
BODY
)
gh issue edit <n> -R {{ github.slug }} --add-label post-mortem
```

No blame, no narrative, no "lessons learned" section. What broke, why, what now
stops it.
